{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 0367d247ca5fc5f1e884c917bc319a1fce554c30, all nine published paths. No blocking findings.\n\nArchitecture: layers 2 (kernel verified history), 4 (application boundary) and 7 (command composition) preserve the contracts of docs/reference/architecture.md@0367d247ca5fc5f1e884c917bc319a1fce554c30. PriorActs is a batch-shaped version of the existing opaque dedup lookup: one captured head, one verified scan and no cache between calls. The application owns the mapping from command keys to namespaced identities. Removing the redundant post-move preflight leaves runBatch's full-chain preflight before any append, including recovery; the pre-target-move gate remains. This changes work cost, not authority or protocol, and needs no architecture-contract amendment.\n\nSecurity: every answer still comes from scanHead's verified records and carries the original act payload. Actor/namespace/key identity and conflicting-intent refusal remain intact. Accepted requests still recover before mutable ref reads; the post-move refusal identifies the actual failed act. A lookup failure cannot authorize an append: sequencing and its post-dedup admission remain authoritative. No unverified cache, custody or signature relaxation, or unresolved trust/authority defect found.\n\nSimplification: one shared plural helper and the retained singular wrapper remove repeated scans without duplicating identity rules. Keeping both explicit succession preflight and runBatch's identical gate would repeat work, not add a boundary. No unnecessary mechanism to remove.\n\nIndependent verification at this head: focused kernel and cmd/gs tests passed (0.726 s and 18.661 s); the additional artifact scan and post-move act-position tests passed (7.473 s). Six existing replay/preflight gates passed unmodified (10.292 s), including no append before full-chain admission, moved-world replay, a new suffix behind an accepted prefix and a whole-chain retry. TestAcceptedRequestIsRecoveredBeforeAnyRefIsRead passed independently (2.099 s). go vet on kernel, app and cmd/gs passed, as did git diff --check. The author's report records a passing full suite; I reran the affected gates rather than claiming an independent full-suite run.\n\nNine compiling omission controls each made a named test red: per-act prospective lookup -\u003e TestBatchCommandReadsTheLogOncePerPreflight; per-act admission lookup -\u003e TestABatchPreflightReadsTheLogOnce; per-path artifact replay lookup -\u003e TestArtifactReadsTheLogOncePerCheck; duplicate succession preflight -\u003e TestMergePreflightsItsSuccessionOnceAfterTheTargetMoves; duplicate incorporation preflight -\u003e TestIncorporationPreflightsItsReceiptOnce; dropping failedPosition -\u003e TestAPostMoveSuccessionRefusalNamesTheAct; dropping payload or returning events under the wrong kernel key -\u003e TestPriorActsAnswersEachKeyWithTheActAppendedUnderIt; returning events under the namespaced application key rather than the caller key -\u003e TestAcceptedRequestIsRecoveredBeforeAnyRefIsRead. The last mutant survived the scan-count test, so I checked the existing request-recovery test, which failed as intended; scan count alone does not prove identity translation.\n\nThe report discloses the frozen artroom comparison (179 whole-log scans before, three after), the prior failed receipt and the remaining append cost. Those are retained author measurements, not a timing campaign I ran. This approval adopts no latency commitment or rollout.\n\nAcknowledged head news: my queue-intake assertion records review order and no approval. It changes no implementation condition.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2198e083fbf651f2956044a30257afd63d5a1b87","binding":"assigned","head":"0367d247ca5fc5f1e884c917bc319a1fce554c30","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6bc34ad07d7cd69312bd230d8167e5634ff0c1ae\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2d